{"code":"def calculate_accuracy(y_true, y_pred):
    if len(y_true) != len(y_pred):
        raise ValueError("y_true and y_pred must have the same length")

    if len(y_true) == 0:
        return 0.0

    correct = 0

    for actual, predicted in zip(y_true, y_pred):
        if actual == predicted:
            correct += 1

    return correct / len(y_true)","status":"pending_evaluation","topic_id":"mlf_009","exercise_id":"mlf_009_accuracy_can_lie_v1","interpretation":"Accuracy can be misleading if there is a bias in the data in itself. For example, if we are measuring the accuracy of the predictions of defect rate and in reality, 95% of the cases are non-defect rates while 5% are defect rates and the model predicts 95% cases as non-defect rates. Then accuracy will be 95% ignoring the fact that it might have failed to detect defects for actual cases.
Hence, relying only on accuracy may lead to business implications of missing on actual defect rates. The better KPI to measure would be recall and procedure. This way we will not just look at how well the model is predicting, but also how accurately it is detecting the defect rates."}

