{"title":"Compute precision and recall for a defect model","passed":true,"topic_id":"checkpoint_ml_foundations_001","next_step":"Good. Now connect the metric result to the production decision and threshold policy.","better_code":"def calculate_precision_recall(y_true, y_pred, positive_label=1):
    tp = fp = fn = 0
    for actual, predicted in zip(y_true, y_pred):
        if predicted == positive_label and actual == positive_label:
            tp += 1
        elif predicted == positive_label and actual != positive_label:
            fp += 1
        elif predicted != positive_label and actual == positive_label:
            fn += 1

    precision = tp / (tp + fp) if (tp + fp) else 0.0
    recall = tp / (tp + fn) if (tp + fn) else 0.0
    return {"precision": precision, "recall": recall}
","exercise_id":"checkpoint_ml_foundations_precision_recall_v1","test_summary":{"passed":true,"total_tests":4,"hidden_total":2,"total_passed":4,"hidden_passed":2,"visible_total":2,"visible_passed":2},"interpretation_score":5,"better_interpretation":"The model may have high overall accuracy because most parts are non-defective, but recall shows whether actual defects are being caught. For a defect detection go-live decision, low recall means false negatives are passing through the system. That is usually more dangerous than a moderate number of false alarms. I would set a minimum defect-class recall threshold, review precision to control inspection workload, and monitor both metrics after deployment.","missing_interpretation_focus":[]}

{"code":"def calculate_precision_recall(y_true, y_pred, positive_label=1):
    if len(y_true) != len(y_pred):
        raise ValueError("y_true and y_pred must have the same length")

    tp = 0
    fp = 0
    fn = 0

    for actual, predicted in zip(y_true, y_pred):
        if predicted == positive_label and actual == positive_label:
            tp += 1
        elif predicted == positive_label and actual != positive_label:
            fp += 1
        elif predicted != positive_label and actual == positive_label:
            fn += 1

    precision_denominator = tp + fp
    recall_denominator = tp + fn

    precision = tp / precision_denominator if precision_denominator > 0 else 0.0
    recall = tp / recall_denominator if recall_denominator > 0 else 0.0

    return {
        "precision": precision,
        "recall": recall
    }","status":"pending_evaluation","topic_id":"checkpoint_ml_foundations_001","exercise_id":"checkpoint_ml_foundations_precision_recall_v1","interpretation":"Both precision and recall are importnant parameters and each evaluate models on different aspects. For example, if we have a ML model to predict defect or no_defect, with positive lable being 1 for defect, then precision shows how well the model was able to predict defects out of the total defects it predicted while precision will evaluate how well the model is detecting defects out of the total actual defects. Both the parameters are important and there has to be a balance between the two. If any one them is tuned more than the other, then we end with business impacts of either defective production or frequent line stoppages. Going by the same example above, if recall is tuned more towards 100%, then it will classify non-defective products as defective and frequently stop the line leading to business loss. If precision is tuned towards 100%, then defective cases will be wrongly classified as non-defective leding to quality issues. We must avoid both and hence in production we should define precision and recall thresholds that are not favouring each other but fives optimum outcome for both. If we see deviations during our monitoring then model needs finetuning. We can also make the threshold definition dynamic based on the values of both the parameters."}

