First, I would define the process to identify the class imbalance and define strategies to minimize it. There are various ways in which we reduce the imbalance each with its tradeoffs. We can use resampling but it changes data distributions. We can optimize class weights but it impacts training penalty and we can implement threshold but it will impact decisions. We will define business and design policies based on cost matrix and FP-FN tradeoffs to identify the optimum control to be implemented. Second, I would validate it using Precision, recall, threshold, business loss and alert-volume costs. Third, I would set monitoring mechanisms to track the shifts in the metrics and define business policy to retrain the model in case of substantial drifts.","question_id":"q4"},{"answer":"Lets assume we have 1000 observations. Out of this, around 900 observations will be about good products while 100 will be defective products. This shows that good observations dominate the dataset. This phenomenon, in which one type of observations dominate the dataset is called as class imbalance. If we build a model on this data and it predicts all the 1000 observations as good, and does not predict any defect, even then the model was able to predict 900 times accurate non-defective cases. The accuracy in this case will be 90%. But, because the model was not able to predict the 100 defective cases, it will lead to quality issues and business losses.","question_id":"q5"}],"topic_id":"mlf_017"}

