{"code":"def metrics_at_threshold(y_true, scores, threshold):
    if len(y_true) != len(scores):
        return {'precision': 0.0, 'recall': 0.0, 'alerts': 0}

    tp = 0
    fp = 0
    fn = 0
    alerts = 0

    for i in range(len(y_true)):
        predicted_positive = scores[i] >= threshold
        actual_positive = y_true[i] == 1

        if predicted_positive:
            alerts += 1

        if predicted_positive and actual_positive:
            tp += 1
        elif predicted_positive and not actual_positive:
            fp += 1
        elif not predicted_positive and actual_positive:
            fn += 1

    if tp + fp == 0:
        precision = 0.0
    else:
        precision = tp / (tp + fp)

    if tp + fn == 0:
        recall = 0.0
    else:
        recall = tp / (tp + fn)

    return {
        'precision': precision,
        'recall': recall,
        'alerts': alerts
    }","status":"pending_evaluation","topic_id":"mlf_023","exercise_id":"mlf_023_metrics_at_threshold_v1","interpretation":"The threshold decides which model scores become defect alerts. For rare defects, I would not select the threshold using recall alone. A lower threshold may catch more actual defects and increase recall, but it can also reduce precision and create more inspection alerts than the quality team can handle. A higher threshold may improve precision and reduce alert volume, but it may miss costly defects. I would choose an operating point that meets the minimum acceptable recall for defect risk while keeping alerts within inspection capacity and considering the business cost of false negatives versus false positives."}

