{"code":"def calculate_precision_recall_f1(y_true, y_pred):
    # Return {'precision': value, 'recall': value, 'f1': value}
    if len(y_true) != len(y_pred) or not y_true:
        return {'precision': 0.0, 'recall': 0.0, 'f1': 0.0}

    tp = 0
    fp = 0
    fn = 0

    for i in range(len(y_true)):
        actual_positive = y_true[i] == 1
        predicted_positive = y_pred[i] == 1

        if predicted_positive and actual_positive:
            tp += 1
        elif predicted_positive and not actual_positive:
            fp += 1
        elif not predicted_positive and actual_positive:
            fn += 1

    precision = 0.0 if (tp + fp) == 0 else tp / (tp + fp)
    recall = 0.0 if (tp + fn) == 0 else tp / (tp + fn)

    if (precision + recall) == 0:
        f1 = 0.0
    else:
        f1 = 2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall)

    return {'precision': precision, 'recall': recall, 'f1': f1}


def choose_threshold(scores, y_true, min_recall=0.80):
    # Predict positive when score >= candidate threshold.
    # Choose the highest threshold from scores meeting minimum recall.
    if len(scores) != len(y_true) or not scores:
        return None

    best_threshold = None

    for threshold in scores:
        tp = 0
        fn = 0

        for i in range(len(scores)):
            predicted_positive = scores[i] >= threshold
            actual_positive = y_true[i] == 1

            if predicted_positive and actual_positive:
                tp += 1
            elif not predicted_positive and actual_positive:
                fn += 1

        recall = 0.0 if (tp + fn) == 0 else tp / (tp + fn)

        if recall >= min_recall:
            if best_threshold is None or threshold > best_threshold:
                best_threshold = threshold

    return best_threshold","status":"pending_evaluation","topic_id":"checkpoint_ml_architect_001","exercise_id":"checkpoint_ml_architect_two_function_gate_v1","interpretation":"I would approve the highest threshold that still meets the required minimum recall, because the recall threshold protects against missing too many true defect cases. A lower threshold may improve recall but increases false positives and alert burden for the inspection team. A higher threshold may reduce alerts and improve precision, but it can miss costly defects. Before approval, I would validate the selected threshold on locked test evidence, review precision, recall, F1, false negatives and alert volume by segment. The ML owner and quality/process owner should monitor recall, precision and alert capacity after deployment and reopen threshold review if performance or workload drifts."}

