{"code":"def calculate_accuracy(y_true, y_pred):
    if len(y_true) == 0:
        return 0.0

    if len(y_true) != len(y_pred):
        raise ValueError("y_true and y_pred must have the same length")

    correct = 0
    for actual, predicted in zip(y_true, y_pred):
        if actual == predicted:
            correct += 1

    return correct / len(y_true)","status":"pending_evaluation","topic_id":"mlf_009","exercise_id":"mlf_009_accuracy_can_lie_v1","interpretation":"High accuracy means the model is correct on many total cases, but it can still miss rare positive cases.
In production, this is risky because the minority class may be the business-critical class, such as defects, fraud, failures, or safety issues.
A model can look strong by predicting the majority class most of the time while producing many false negatives.
False negatives and false positives may not have the same business cost, so accuracy alone is not enough.
I would add a confusion matrix, recall, precision, and class-specific metrics before approving the model for production use."}
